  "response": "## Setting Up the Calculation\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem:\n- Prevalence: P(condition) = 1/500 = 0.002\n- Sensitivity: P(positive | condition) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(negative | no condition) = 0.95 → P(positive | no condition) = 0.05\n\n**Numerator** (true positives):\nP(condition) × P(positive | condition) = 0.002 × 0.98 = 0.00196\n\n**Denominator** (all positives):\nP(positive) = 0.00196 + (0.998 × 0.05) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\n**Posterior probability:**\nP(condition | positive) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\nConverting to a percentage: **3.7794%**, which rounds to **3.78%**.\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly good test, because the condition is rare, most positive results are false positives.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
